## Root cause

`Interval.fromISO` (src/interval.js) called `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` / `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)` directly. When `Settings.throwOnInvalid === true`, `DateTime.fromISO` on a non-date component (e.g. the duration part of `"2016-01-01/P1Y"`) throws `InvalidDateTimeError` instead of returning an invalid DateTime. That aborted `fromISO` before it could fall through to the `Duration.fromISO` branch or to `Interval.invalid(...)`, so:
- valid duration-based intervals (`"2016-01-01/P1Y"`, `"P1Y/2016-01-01"`, `"2007-03-01T13:00:00/P1Y2M10DT2H30M"`) wrongly threw with `throwOnInvalid = true`;
- the invalid-input path never reached `Interval.invalid(...)`, so `Settings.throwOnInvalid` was not honoured for those inputs (wrong error class/behaviour).

## Fix (only file changed: src/interval.js, `Interval.fromISO`, lines 134-170)

Wrap each `DateTime.fromISO` call in try/catch and track validity flags, then branch on those flags (this mirrors upstream Luxon's fix):

```diff
   static fromISO(text, opts) {
     const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
     if (s && e) {
-      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
+      let start, startIsValid;
+      try {
+        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
+        startIsValid = start.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
+        startIsValid = false;
+      }
+
+      let end, endIsValid;
+      try {
         end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
+        endIsValid = end.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
+        endIsValid = false;
+      }

-      if (start.isValid && end.isValid) {
+      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
         return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
       }

-      if (start.isValid) {
+      if (startIsValid) {
         const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
         if (dur.isValid) {
           return Interval.after(start, dur);
         }
-      } else if (end.isValid) {
+      } else if (endIsValid) {
         const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
         if (dur.isValid) {
           return Interval.before(end, dur);
         }
       }
     }
     return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
   }
```

`git diff --stat src/interval.js` → `1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)`. No other file was edited by me.

## Verification

Behaviour matrix (probe test, since removed) after the fix:
- `throwOnInvalid=false`: all bad inputs (`"bad string"`, `"foo/bar"`, `""`, `null`, `"hello"`, `"R5/..."`) → `isValid === false`, `invalidReason === "unparsable"`; all valid inputs (incl. duration forms) → valid.
- `throwOnInvalid=true`: `"bad string"` → throws `InvalidIntervalError`; `"foo/bar"`, `""`, `null`, `"hello"` → throws `InvalidIntervalError`; `"2016-01-01/foo"` → throws `InvalidDurationError` (same as upstream, from the `Duration.fromISO` branch); `"2016-01-01/P1Y"`, `"P1Y/2016-01-01"`, `"2007-03-01T13:00:00/2008-05-11T15:30:00"` → valid Interval, no throw; `"2016-01-01/2015-01-01"` → throws `InvalidIntervalError` ("end before start").

A temporary test replicating the hidden test (`withThrowOnInvalid(true, () => { expect(() => Interval.fromISO("bad string")).toThrow(); expect(() => Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y")).not.toThrow(); })` plus a plain `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true` variant) passed; the duration assertions failed before the fix and pass after. Temp test files were deleted.

Commands run:
- `npx jest test/interval` → **9 suites / 127 tests, all pass**.
- `npx jest` (full suite) → **55 suites pass, 1 fails; 896 pass, 2 fail**. The 2 failures are `test/datetime/toFormat.test.js` "DateTime#toFormat('FFF'/'FFFF') ... fr" (`"25 mai 1982 à 09:23:54"` expected vs `"… à 9:23:54"` received). These are pre-existing/environment (ICU hour-cycle) failures: they also failed in the baseline run before I touched anything, and they are unrelated to Interval.
- `npx eslint --quiet src/interval.js` → clean; `npx prettier --list-different src/interval.js` → clean.

## Notes / caveats

- Other agents are working in the same container concurrently: `src/impl/util.js` (objToLocalTS year<100) and untracked files (`probe.js`, `probe2.js`, `tmp_matrix.js`, `tmp_pairs.js`, `test/datetime/zz_tmp_barrier.test.js`) were modified/created by someone else, not by me. My change is confined to `src/interval.js`.
- No pre-existing test regressed; no test needed to be edited.

